Feature: SQL Transformation Logic Enhancement for purgo_playground.pat_account Table

  The SQL logic for the purgo_playground.pat_account table must be updated to:
  - Modify transformation rules for prescriber_id, patient_sf_id, and hash_key columns.
  - Implement new logic for last_modified_date column.
  - Ensure all changes align with the latest business requirements and mapping rules in source_target_mapping_specifications.xlsx.
  - Guarantee data quality, consistency, and downstream reporting accuracy.

  Background:
    Given the source table purgo_playground.p_account contains columns:
      | patient_foundation_shipment | prescriber_name_c | prescriber_name_c_address | patinet_c | recordtypeid | id | accountid |
    And the target table purgo_playground.pat_account contains columns:
      | patient_foundation_shipment | prescriber_id | prescriber_key | patient_sf_id | service_request_type | case_sf_id | account_id | hash_key | last_modified_date |
    And the mapping rules are defined in source_target_mapping_specifications.xlsx

  # Happy Path Scenarios

  Scenario: Transform prescriber_id with uppercase and conditional "DR." prefix
    Given a source record with prescriber_name_c = "john smith"
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then prescriber_id in the target table should be "DR.JOHN SMITH"

  Scenario: Transform prescriber_id when "DR." prefix already present (case insensitive)
    Given a source record with prescriber_name_c = "Dr. Jane Doe"
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then prescriber_id in the target table should be "DR. JANE DOE"

  Scenario: Transform prescriber_id with mixed case and no prefix
    Given a source record with prescriber_name_c = "jAnE dOe"
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then prescriber_id in the target table should be "DR.JANE DOE"

  Scenario Outline: Data-driven test for prescriber_id transformation
    Given a source record with prescriber_name_c = <input>
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then prescriber_id in the target table should be <expected>
    Examples:
      | input           | expected         |
      | "john smith"    | "DR.JOHN SMITH" |
      | "DR. JOHN SMITH"| "DR. JOHN SMITH"|
      | "dr. john smith"| "DR. JOHN SMITH"|
      | "Jane Doe"      | "DR.JANE DOE"   |
      | "DR.Jane Doe"   | "DR. JANE DOE"  |

  Scenario: Transform patient_sf_id when patinet_c contains "PAT"
    Given a source record with patinet_c = "PAT12345"
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then patient_sf_id in the target table should be "PAT12345"

  Scenario: Set patient_sf_id to NULL when patinet_c does not contain "PAT"
    Given a source record with patinet_c = "12345"
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then patient_sf_id in the target table should be NULL

  Scenario Outline: Data-driven test for patient_sf_id transformation
    Given a source record with patinet_c = <input>
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then patient_sf_id in the target table should be <expected>
    Examples:
      | input      | expected    |
      | "PAT001"   | "PAT001"    |
      | "pat002"   | "pat002"    |
      | "123PAT"   | "123PAT"    |
      | "12345"    | NULL        |
      | "PATTY"    | "PATTY"     |
      | "PAT"      | "PAT"       |
      | "APAT"     | "APAT"      |
      | "XYZ"      | NULL        |

  Scenario: Transform hash_key by concatenating patinet_c, recordtypeid, and id with underscores
    Given a source record with patinet_c = "PAT123", recordtypeid = "RT01", id = "ID99"
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then hash_key in the target table should be "PAT123_RT01_ID99"

  Scenario: Exclude accountid from hash_key concatenation
    Given a source record with patinet_c = "PAT456", recordtypeid = "RT02", id = "ID88", accountid = "ACC77"
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then hash_key in the target table should be "PAT456_RT02_ID88"

  Scenario Outline: Data-driven test for hash_key transformation
    Given a source record with patinet_c = <patinet_c>, recordtypeid = <recordtypeid>, id = <id>
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then hash_key in the target table should be <expected>
    Examples:
      | patinet_c | recordtypeid | id    | expected           |
      | "PAT1"    | "RT1"        | "ID1" | "PAT1_RT1_ID1"     |
      | "PAT2"    | "RT2"        | "ID2" | "PAT2_RT2_ID2"     |
      | "123PAT"  | "RT3"        | "ID3" | "123PAT_RT3_ID3"   |
      | "XYZ"     | "RT4"        | "ID4" | "XYZ_RT4_ID4"      |

  Scenario: Set last_modified_date to current timestamp for each inserted row
    Given a source record with any values
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then last_modified_date in the target table should be set to the current timestamp

  # Error Scenarios

  Scenario: Error when prescriber_name_c is NULL
    Given a source record with prescriber_name_c = NULL
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then prescriber_id in the target table should be NULL

  Scenario: Error when patinet_c is NULL
    Given a source record with patinet_c = NULL
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then patient_sf_id in the target table should be NULL
    And hash_key in the target table should be "_<recordtypeid>_<id>"

  Scenario: Error when recordtypeid is NULL
    Given a source record with recordtypeid = NULL
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then hash_key in the target table should be "<patinet_c>__<id>"

  Scenario: Error when id is NULL
    Given a source record with id = NULL
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then hash_key in the target table should be "<patinet_c>_<recordtypeid>_"

  Scenario Outline: Data-driven test for hash_key error handling
    Given a source record with patinet_c = <patinet_c>, recordtypeid = <recordtypeid>, id = <id>
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then hash_key in the target table should be <expected>
    Examples:
      | patinet_c | recordtypeid | id    | expected           |
      | NULL      | "RT1"        | "ID1" | "_RT1_ID1"         |
      | "PAT2"    | NULL         | "ID2" | "PAT2__ID2"        |
      | "PAT3"    | "RT3"        | NULL  | "PAT3_RT3_"        |
      | NULL      | NULL         | NULL  | "__"               |

  Scenario: Error when last_modified_date column is missing in target table schema
    Given the target table schema does not include last_modified_date
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then the process should fail with error message "Column 'last_modified_date' does not exist in target table schema"

  Scenario: Error when source columns are missing
    Given the source table is missing prescriber_name_c, patinet_c, recordtypeid, or id
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then the process should fail with error message "Required source column(s) missing: <column_name>"

  # Validation Rules

  Scenario: Validate prescriber_id is always uppercase and prefixed with "DR." if not present
    Given a source record with prescriber_name_c = <input>
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then prescriber_id in the target table should match the regex "^DR\.[A-Z ]+$"

  Scenario: Validate patient_sf_id is NULL if patinet_c does not contain "PAT"
    Given a source record with patinet_c = <input>
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then patient_sf_id in the target table should be NULL if <input> does not contain "PAT"

  Scenario: Validate hash_key format
    Given a source record with patinet_c, recordtypeid, and id
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then hash_key in the target table should match the regex "^[^_]*_[^_]*_[^_]*$"

  Scenario: Validate last_modified_date is a valid timestamp
    Given a source record with any values
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then last_modified_date in the target table should be a valid timestamp in ISO 8601 format

  # Downstream Impact

  Scenario: Validate downstream reporting tables receive updated last_modified_date
    Given a record is inserted into purgo_playground.pat_account with last_modified_date set
    When downstream reporting ETL runs
    Then the corresponding record in purgo_playground.pact_case should have last_modified_date matching the source

  # Schema Alignment

  Scenario: Validate target table schema includes last_modified_date as timestamp
    Given the target table schema is defined
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then the schema should include last_modified_date with datatype timestamp

  # Error Message Validation

  Scenario Outline: Error message validation for missing columns
    Given the source table is missing <column>
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then the process should fail with error message "Required source column(s) missing: <column>"
    Examples:
      | column              |
      | prescriber_name_c   |
      | patinet_c           |
      | recordtypeid        |
      | id                  |

  Scenario: Error message validation for missing last_modified_date in target schema
    Given the target table schema does not include last_modified_date
    When the SQL transformation is executed
    Then the process should fail with error message "Column 'last_modified_date' does not exist in target table schema"
